    {
      "id": "ccna-rest-change-request",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "CCNA",
      "topic": "REST network changes",
      "unit": "rest-network-changes",
      "title": "Repair an authorized network change request",
      "summary": "Read a resource, diagnose authorization and JSON errors, then verify the network.",
      "objective": "Restore communication between the two staff endpoints by repairing the staff profile. Keep its current description. Keep both guest endpoints connected and separated from staff. Keep the visitors profile unchanged.",
      "retry_guidance": "Read the actual response first. Correct the request fields and send again; then use each failing network requirement to check the applied result.",
      "difficulty": "Beginner",
      "minutes": 25,
      "prerequisites": [],
      "steps": [
        "Read before changing",
        "A request has separate parts",
        "Permission and JSON types matter",
        "Response success is not task success"
      ],
      "guide": [
        {
          "title": "Read before changing",
          "why": "A resource is a named piece of data. GET /profiles lists the profiles; GET /profiles/team reads one profile. A binding selects which profile controls a supplied port. Changing a shared profile affects every port using it.",
          "action": "Use the native request controls and inspect the actual response.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "A request has separate parts",
          "why": "Method says what to do; Path selects the resource; Authorization identifies who is asking; Content-Type describes the body. GET reads. PATCH changes named fields. PUT replaces the full representation, so include required fields and any description you need to retain.",
          "action": "Use the native request controls and inspect the actual response.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Permission and JSON types matter",
          "why": "This simulator supplies Bearer lab-reader for reading and Bearer lab-operator for changes. A missing or invalid identity gets 401; a reader attempting a change gets 403. These labels are not real credentials. JSON true is an on/off value; \"true\" is text. PATCH uses application/merge-patch+json. A rejected request leaves resources unchanged.",
          "action": "Use the native request controls and inspect the actual response.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Response success is not task success",
          "why": "Send request applies one valid change. A 204 response means this origin accepted the update; it does not prove the desired network works. Check network separately tests current paths, group separation and protected fields. Editing request fields clears the current result; sending a change can break a previously working network.",
          "action": "Use the native request controls and inspect the actual response.",
          "commands": []
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The request needed a permitted identity, a matching media type and a real boolean. PATCH retained the other profile fields while enabling both selected ports. Equivalent complete PUT representations can work too. Only the network and preservation checks established the requested outcome; a2xx status alone did not.",
      "hints": [
        "Use GET /profiles and GET /bindings with an empty Body to inspect current resources. The supplied map connects binding names to switch ports and endpoints.",
        "401 means identity missing or invalid; 403 means identified but not permitted; 415 means wrong Content-Type; 400 means a JSON, path or request-format error; 422 means the data violates this resource schema.",
        "A 2xx response is not completion. Check network, read each failing requirement, and keep unrelated profile fields intact."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://learningcontent.cisco.com/documents/marketing/exam-topics/200-301-CCNA-v1.1.pdf",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7396.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Sources checked 2026-11-13UTC. Finite in-process lab origin, not a Cisco API or HTTP wire implementation. Supplied synthetic Bearer roles are not real credentials, Basic, OAuth or TLS. Named profiles and fixed access-port bindings project into existing Ethernet/VLAN simulation. GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE only; JSON and Merge Patch with typed resource limits. No URL, server, socket, shell or host execution. Up to12profiles/12bindings,4KiB request bodies,128JSON values/depth8,64changes/16KiB replay and64KiB session. See REST_CONTROLLER_SUBSET.md. No complete6.5or controller architecture claim.",
      "initial": {
